TY  - JOUR
AU  - Kubes, Tanja
PY  - 2026
TI  - A Critique of Human-Centred AI: A Plea for a Feminist AI Framework (FAIF)
JO  - AI & Society
VL  - 41
IS  - 4
SP  - 2827
EP  - 2838
DO  - 10.1007/s00146-025-02556-8
UR  - https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00146-025-02556-8
AB  - Kubes argues that Human-Centred AI (HCAI), widely hailed as advancing fairness, equity and inclusion, in fact remains Eurocentric, androcentric and anthropocentric and is driven by capitalist market logic without a sustainability commitment, thereby reproducing global injustices and ecological harm. Drawing on feminist science and technology studies, posthumanism, neo-materialism, more-than-human anthropology and body-phenomenology, she proposes a Feminist AI Framework (FAIF) that reconfigures conceptions of the human, intelligence, agency and technological relations beyond human exceptionalism. The paper frames the problem as structural rather than solvable through technical progress alone, aligning with the research question's emphasis on power asymmetries and the insufficiency of individualised competence framings.
N1  - L2: Quality: HIGH. (a) Peer-reviewed article (open access, CC BY). (b) AI & Society (Springer) is a long-standing, reputable journal in AI, ethics and society; per Journal Citation Reports it had a 2022 impact factor of 3.0 (CiteScore 9.8; some 2024 Scopus-based estimates are higher, around 6). (c) Theoretical/conceptual contribution; robust within critical-theory scholarship though not empirical. (d) Published online 19 August 2025, in print volume 41(4) 2026; no verifiable citation count could be located (likely near zero). A correction (10.1007/s00146-025-02718-8) is attached. (e) Well-argued and highly relevant to feminist STS applied to AI and structural power critique.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
AU  - Andryani, Nur Afny Catur
PY  - 2025
TI  - AI Literacy and Gender Bias: Comparative Perspectives from the UK and Indonesia
JO  - Education Sciences
VL  - 15
IS  - 9
SP  - 1143
DO  - 10.3390/educsci15091143
UR  - https://www.mdpi.com/2227-7102/15/9/1143
AB  - This survey study of 192 participants from the United Kingdom and Indonesia compares AI literacy and perceptions of gender bias. Overall AI literacy levels are similar across countries, but UK respondents report significantly higher familiarity with programming and AI technologies, reflecting differences in educational frameworks and infrastructure. Despite widespread AI use, ethical discussion remains limited in both contexts, and persistent gender biases affect women's participation and progression in AI and STEM. The study highlights cultural variation in perceptions of bias in recruitment, leadership and support for women. It offers evidence relevant to debates about whether individual literacy/competence interventions can address gendered structural inequalities, implicitly showing the limits of competence-based framings absent structural change.
N1  - L2: Quality: MEDIUM. (a) Peer-reviewed (MDPI open access). (b) Education Sciences is an indexed MDPI journal; per the June 2026 Journal Citation Reports its impact factor is 3.5 (its first IF was 3.0 in 2023), of moderate standing, and MDPI's rapid-review model attracts some scholarly debate. (c) Survey methodology with self-report measures and a modest, non-probability sample (n=192); descriptive/comparative rather than causal. (d) Published 2 September 2025; citation count not yet established. (e) Clear and relevant to AI literacy and gender, though it treats literacy largely as individual competence and engages structural/intersectional critique less deeply than the core feminist-STS sources.
