TY  - JOUR
AU  - Koobak, Redi
PY  - 2026
TI  - Feminist knowledges in the age of AI
JO  - European Journal of Women's Studies
VL  - 33
IS  - 1
SP  - 3
EP  - 5
DO  - 10.1177/13505068261419933
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1177/13505068261419933
AB  - In this editorial, Koobak argues that the AI infrastructures increasingly mediating scholarship, submission platforms, citation databases and large language models, are not neutral but embedded in an extractive "technological empire" (drawing on Karen Hao) that reshapes what counts as legitimate feminist knowledge. She calls for feminist scholarship to treat computational systems as objects of critique within knowledge production and to resist the naturalisation of these tools. The piece frames AI literacy and critique as collective, structural concerns rather than matters of individual skill, aligning with the research question's tension between competence and power.
N1  - L2: Quality: MEDIUM. (a) Published in a peer-reviewed journal but is itself an EDITORIAL, not a peer-reviewed research article. (b) European Journal of Women's Studies (SAGE) is a well-established gender-studies journal; per Clarivate JCR released 17 June 2026 (2025 data) its impact factor is 1.7 (JCR Q2; SAGE lists 1.8, 5-year 2.2). (c) No empirical methodology; a short agenda-setting essay. (d) Published online 30 January 2026; Web of Science and Crossref show 0 citations. (e) Concise and thematically relevant, but brief and non-empirical; best used as framing rather than evidence.
